                <pb facs="0295" n="175" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg175"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">816.00 Revolutions/22½: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in El Salvador</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Curtis</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-12-05T13:00:00-05:00">December 5, 1931—1
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>60. It appears from your telegrams that the revolution was organized and carried
                    out by younger army officers. You also mention, however, that General Martínez,
                    who is presumably identical with Vice President Martínez, is assisting actively
                    in the movement.</p>
                <p>Please report fully by telegraph as to part played in the revolution by the Vice
                    President and by the three <hi rendition="#italic">designados</hi> and whether
                    any of the latter are still in San Salvador, or whether they accompanied
                    President Araujo to Santa Ana.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
